# Source: Exercise list "ICT3101 Exercises - Deductive Verification", problem 4 b), with its solution sheet.
id: prove-sequence-b
title: Prove a triple with logical variables (b)
topic: Sequential composition
level: 2
source: Exercise list "HoareLogic Exercises", problem 4 b
tags: assignment, sequence, logical variables, signs

task:
  Prove the following Hoare triple.

pre: x = x' && y = y'
post: x = -m && y = x' + n + y'

program:
  {P}
  {R3}
  x := x + y;            // i1
  {R2}
  y := x + n;            // i2
  {R1}
  x := x - y + n - m     // i3
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The last statement, `x := x - y + n - m`
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i3
calc:
  wp(x := x - y + n - m, Q)
  = (x = -m && y = x' + n + y')[x <- x - y + n - m]
  = x - y + n - m = -m && y = x' + n + y'
set: R1 = x - y + n - m = -m && y = x' + n + y'

step: The second statement, `y := x + n`
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
text:
  The variable $y$ occurs twice in $R1$, so it is replaced twice. The
  parentheses matter: the minus sign in front of $y$ applies to the whole
  expression $x + n$.
calc:
  wp(y := x + n, R1)
  = x - (x + n) + n - m = -m && x + n = x' + n + y'
  = x - x - n + n - m = -m && x + n = x' + n + y'    // open the parentheses: the minus changes both signs
  = -m = -m && x = x' + y'
  = x = x' + y'                                       // -m = -m is true, and true && A is A
set: R2 = x = x' + y'

step: The first statement, `x := x + y`
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
calc:
  wp(x := x + y, R2)
  = (x = x' + y')[x <- x + y]
  = x + y = x' + y'
set: R3 = x + y = x' + y'

step: Compare with the precondition
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
vc: x = x' && y = y' ==> x + y = x' + y'
why:
  Replace $x$ by $x'$ and $y$ by $y'$ using the assumption: the right-hand
  side becomes $x' + y' = x' + y'$, which is true.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  The most frequent mistake in this exercise is the sign error
  $x - (x + n) = x - x + n$. The correct expansion is $x - x - n$, and
  this is what makes the terms $n$ cancel.
